Return in try
public static int test(){

    try{
        return 10;
    }
    finally{
        System.out.println("Finally");
    }
}
Output

Finally
10
The return value is computed first, then the finally block runs before the method actually returns.

Return in finally (Not Recommended)
public static int test(){

    try{
        return 10;
    }
    finally{
        return 20;
    }
}
Output

20
The finally return overrides the try return. This is considered bad practice because it hides exceptions and makes code harder to understand.

3. finalize() Method
Important: finalize() is deprecated (since Java 9) and is intended for removal. You should not use it in modern Java.

It was defined in Object:

protected void finalize() throws Throwable
The idea was that the JVM might call it before garbage collecting an object.

Example:

class Demo{

    @Override
    protected void finalize(){

        System.out.println("Finalize called");
    }

    public static void main(String args[]){

        Demo d=new Demo();

        d=null;

        System.gc();
    }
}
Possible Output

Finalize called
or

(no output)
Why?

Because there is no guarantee that finalize() will ever run. System.gc() is only a request to the JVM, not a command.

Why was finalize() removed?
Problems:

Unpredictable execution.

Performance overhead.

Objects survive longer than necessary.

Security concerns.

Resource leaks if developers relied on it.

Instead, Java recommends:

try-with-resources

Explicit close() methods

AutoCloseable

Example:

try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(br.readLine());
}
The resource is closed automatically, even if an exception occurs.

final vs finally vs finalize()
Feature	final	finally	finalize()
Type	Keyword	Block	Method
Purpose	Restrict modification	Cleanup after try/catch	Legacy cleanup before garbage collection
Used With	Variables, methods, classes	Exception handling	Object class
Execution	Compile-time restriction	Runs after try/catch (except in rare cases like System.exit())	No execution guarantee
Current Status	Widely used	Widely used	Deprecated; avoid using it
