Excellent question. This is exactly the kind of question asked in interviews.

The short answer is:

try-with-resources has NO relation with finalize(). They solve different problems.

Let's understand it from the JVM's perspective.

Before Java 7 (Without try-with-resources)

Suppose you open a file.

FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt");

// Read data

fis.close();

Now imagine an exception occurs before close().

FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt");

int x = 10 / 0;   // Exception

fis.close();      // Never executes

Now the file remains open.

This causes a resource leak.

Developers thought:

"Don't worry, finalize() will close it."

For example:

class MyFile {

    FileInputStream fis;

    @Override
    protected void finalize() {
        System.out.println("Closing file");
    }
}

But this is a bad idea.

Why?

Because nobody knows when finalize() will run.

Object becomes unreachable
        ↓
Garbage Collector may run
        ↓
JVM may call finalize()
        ↓
Object removed

Notice the word may.

It may happen after:

1 second
1 minute
10 minutes
Never (before the JVM exits)

Meanwhile the file stays open.

Why is this dangerous?

Suppose your server handles 10,000 requests.

Each request opens a file.

Open File
↓

Wait for finalize()

↓

Open File

↓

Wait for finalize()

↓

Open File

Eventually you'll have thousands of open files.

Then the operating system says:

Too many open files

Your application starts failing.

Java 7 introduced try-with-resources

Instead of waiting for the Garbage Collector,

Java says:

"Close the resource immediately."

Example

try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt")) {

    // Read file

}

When execution leaves the try block...

Normal execution
        ↓
close()

or

Exception occurs
        ↓
close()

Either way,

close() is automatically called.

No Garbage Collector involved.

Who calls close()?

The compiler generates code similar to this:

FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt");

try {

    // use file

} finally {

    if (fis != null) {
        fis.close();
    }

}

So it's the compiler-generated finally block that calls close(), not finalize().

Then why doesn't it call finalize()?

Because finalize() depends on the Garbage Collector.

Garbage Collection is memory cleanup.

Closing a file is resource cleanup.

These are different things.

Imagine:

RAM Memory
↓

Garbage Collector
↓

Object deleted

versus

Operating System File
↓

close()

↓

File released

The Garbage Collector only knows about Java objects in memory. It doesn't know when your program should release an operating system resource like a file, socket, or database connection.

What is the relation then?

There is almost no direct relation.

Historically:

Old approach

Open Resource
      ↓
Forget to close
      ↓
Hope finalize() closes it
      ↓
Unreliable

Modern approach:

Open Resource
      ↓
try-with-resources
      ↓
Compiler generates finally
      ↓
close()
      ↓
Done

So try-with-resources replaced the need to rely on finalize() for releasing resources.

How does try-with-resources know to call close()?

Because the resource must implement the AutoCloseable interface (or its subinterface Closeable).

public interface AutoCloseable {
    void close() throws Exception;
}

FileInputStream implements Closeable, which extends AutoCloseable:

AutoCloseable
       ↑
   Closeable
       ↑
FileInputStream

So when you write:

try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("data.txt")) {
    // use file
}

the compiler knows that fis has a close() method and generates the cleanup code automatically.